# ── Sakhi AI environment variables ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to your environment / Keys UI. Never commit real values.

# Supabase (client — safe to expose in the browser)
# Get these from: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ── Development accounts only ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required to run `bun run seed:dev`, which creates the three dev accounts
# (aanya@gmail.com / guardian@gmail.com / admin@sakhi.dev) with known
# passwords so development isn't blocked on email/OTP. The service role key
# bypasses Row Level Security — NEVER prefix it with VITE_ (it must never
# reach the browser bundle) and never use it in production code.
# Get it from: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role secret
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# NOTE: normal sign-in is EMAIL OR MOBILE + PASSWORD via Supabase Auth.
# Mobile numbers are resolved to the account email by the
# lookup_email_by_phone RPC (run supabase/migrations/20260816000000_email_or_phone_login.sql
# in the SQL Editor). Account creation uses Supabase's built-in email
# verification: signUp() sends a "Confirm signup" email with a verification
# LINK (no OTP anywhere). No extra secrets are needed in the app, but the
# Supabase project must be configured once (Dashboard → Auth):
#   * Email provider enabled + "Confirm email" ON (Auth → Providers → Email).
#   * Site URL set to this app's URL (Auth → URL Configuration) so the
#     verification link redirects back to the app.
#   * "Allow new users to sign up" enabled (Auth → Sign In / Up).
#   * Custom SMTP toggle OFF in Auth → SMTP (use the built-in provider — no
#     credentials needed; emails come from no-reply@supabase.co). If sign-up
#     shows "Error sending confirmation email" (HTTP 500), Custom SMTP is on
#     with a broken key/sender — turn it OFF.
#
# Password recovery uses Supabase's Reset Password email (a link that opens
# the app's /reset-password screen).
#
# The mobile number is collected during onboarding and stored on the user's
# profile (for a future SMS OTP migration and SOS alerts), but it is never
# used to send the login code.
